swehouse.c: fix APC cusps near the equator

apc_sector() forms the declination of the Ascendant only to use it in the
product tan(dasc) * tan(ph). By the ascensional difference relation
sin(kv) = tan(ph) * tan(dasc), that product is sin(kv), and kv is already in
hand. Computing dasc = atan(sin(kv) / tan(ph)) and multiplying by tan(ph)
again is a round trip that becomes 0/0 on the equator, where sin(kv) and
tan(ph) vanish together.

The old code broke the tie by substituting the constant 90 - VERY_SMALL for
dasc whenever |latitude| < 1e-10 degrees. tan() of that constant is about
5.7e11, so the product came out roughly 2e12 times too large and grew with
latitude across the band instead of vanishing with it. The cusps it returned
there were not the APC cusps: just inside the band, at 9.975e-11 degrees of
latitude, cusp 1 sat up to 48.5 degrees from the Ascendant and cusps 1 and 7
up to 94.8 degrees from opposite - each figure its own worst armc, they do
not co-occur at one chart - where the definition requires the Ascendant and
the Descendant exactly. The test is strict, so at 1e-10 exactly
the old code was already correct; the damage is confined to what lies below.

Taking sin(kv) directly removes the round trip and the special case together.

Verified: across the former band, from 1e-14 degrees of latitude up, cusps 1
and 7 oppose to within 1.5e-13 degrees everywhere, and cusp 1 sits within
1.5e-13 of the Ascendant away from the armc quadrants - a few ULP at the
demonstration chart, thousands at the worst armc of a full sweep. Within
about 1e-8 degrees of an exact quadrant the difference grows: about 1e-9 a
nanodegree off the quadrant, about 6e-9 at the window's edge. That is the generic
Ascendant's own snap-to-axis clamp showing through, not this function: at
armc 180 + 1e-9 and the band's floor of 1e-14 latitude, the cusp reads the
analytically exact 270.00000000091745 while ascmc[0] snaps flat to 270. All
of it sits far below anything swetest prints.
Outside the band the two forms agree to within a few units in the last
place, 3.4e-16 in the product over the samples taken, and cusp positions move
by at most 2.7e-10 degrees over 24,263,712 samples spanning latitudes -89.5 to
89.5 and a full armc sweep, always toward the exact value. No non-finite
result anywhere.

There is a check that needs no separate implementation at all. On the equator
kv is zero, so sin(kv) is zero and the formula collapses to the ecliptic
longitudes of right ascensions armc + 90 + 30k: APC at latitude zero is the
Meridian system, which this file already computes independently as case 'X'.
The corrected apc_sector() reproduces case 'X' at latitude zero to within
2e-13 degrees on every cusp - below anything swetest prints, though not to
the bit; both builds carry that rounding equally - and approaches it linearly
on the way in, 4e-13 at 1e-12 degrees and 3.7e-9 at 1e-8. The old code was 48
degrees away from it inside the band.
Anyone can repeat this with two calls to swe_houses_armc_ex2().

The identity itself was checked against the definition rather than assumed:
the declination of the Ascendant taken from the standard formula reproduces
sin(kv) = tan(ph) * tan(dasc) to 1e-40 in 40 digit arithmetic, at every
latitude and armc, so the substitution is exact as astronomy and not only as
algebra.

The sign flip of kv inside the polar circle, which the comment above it calls
out as intentional, is untouched: the identity holds there too. That comment,
the debug printf beside it and the note on the position circle further down
all named dasc, and are reworded here, since the variable is gone.

Checked against an independent implementation built from the definition rather
than from this file: the parallel of declination through the Ascendant divided
into six parts each side of the horizon, position circles through the north and
south points of the horizon carried through the division points by cross
products, intersected with the ecliptic. Below the polar circle that
construction agrees with apc_sector() cusp by cusp to 1e-13 at every latitude
tried, and it agrees with the corrected version inside the former clamp band to
2e-13 where it disagrees with the old one by up to 6.2 degrees.

Above the polar circle the reference and apc_sector() diverge, and the
reference is the one at fault. Each position circle meets the ecliptic twice,
and picking the right one of the two becomes ill conditioned there: near the
degenerate configurations, where the Ascendant and the MC close to within a
fraction of a degree and all twelve cusps crowd together, the vector
construction picks the wrong intersection for individual cusps. A test that
needs no reference at all settles it. The twelve cusps must run round the
circle once in a single direction; over 973 samples per latitude apc_sector()
does so at every latitude out to 89 degrees, 973 of 973 each time, while the
vector construction fails in up to 138 of 973 beyond the polar circle. Its
disagreement there is therefore not evidence about apc_sector(). The direction
apc_sector() runs in beyond the polar circle is clockwise, which is what
swisseph.doc section 6.4 prescribes: the Ascendant is kept in the eastern
hemisphere and the MC and IC are swapped when the MC falls below the horizon.

The equator clamp is not, however, the only defect this file has in APC. It is
the one this patch corrects. A second remains, of the same shape and left alone
here. Where the ecliptic coincides with the horizon, that is geographic
latitude on a polar circle with armc on 90 or 270 degrees, tan(ph) * tan(e) is
exactly -1 and the factor 1 + tan(ph) * tan(e) * sin(az) inside kv cancels
away. Within about 1e-4 degrees of that configuration the cusps apc_sector()
returns are wrong: by 0.01 degrees at 1e-4 from it, by 1 degree at 1e-5, by 222
degrees at 1e-6, erratically rather than monotonically, as cancellation is.
Measured against 50 digit arithmetic.

Inside that band this patch does change the values, and an earlier draft of
this paragraph wrongly said it did not. The rewritten kv is the same function
along a different rounding path, and where every digit is being lost a
different rounding path lands somewhere else: at the Arctic Circle with the
J2000 obliquity the two libraries differ out to about 3e-3 degrees of armc
around the coincidence, by up to 180 degrees at its centre. Neither is right
there. Sampled against 60 digit arithmetic over 48,000 cusp values inside the
band, the patched value is the closer one in 1,319 and the further one in 829,
so the change is noise on top of noise rather than a repair or a break; the
2.7e-10 bound quoted above holds everywhere outside this band and outside the
equator band this patch is about.

The repair is not the one used here. sin(kv) does not help, because it is kv
itself that loses its digits rather than a round trip around it, and a
formulation that is stable there and still agrees with the present one
elsewhere has not been found; four attempts and their failures are recorded in
the companion patch's notes.

The remark above case 'Y' that the MC from apc_sector() is inaccurate near
latitude 90 is not a live defect: CalcH() overwrites cusp 10 with hsp->mc and
cusp 4 with its opposite immediately afterwards.

apc_sector_speed(), added by the analytic cusp speed patch, already
differentiates the corrected form, so the two patches agree once both are
applied and APC speeds reach 1e-11 at every latitude.

This patch is independent of the analytic cusp speed patch and the two may be
applied in either order, and produce the same file whichever order is used.

---
 swehouse.c | 32 +++++++++++++++++---------------
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)
diff --git a/swehouse.c b/swehouse.c
index 1c5bbd3..e67955a 100644
--- a/swehouse.c
+++ b/swehouse.c
@@ -782,26 +782,28 @@ for (i = 1; i <=12; i++) {
 static double apc_sector(int n, double ph, double e, double az)
 {
    int k, is_below_hor = 0;
-   double kv, a, dasc, dret;
+   double kv, a, skv, dret;
    /* kv: ascensional difference of the ascendant */
-   /* dasc: declination of the ascendant */
    if (fabs(ph * RADTODEG) > 90 - VERY_SMALL) {
      kv = 0;
-     dasc = 0;
    } else {
      kv   = atan(tan(ph) * tan(e) * cos(az)/(1 + tan(ph) * tan(e) * sin(az)));
-     if (fabs(ph * RADTODEG) < VERY_SMALL) {
-       dasc = (90 - VERY_SMALL) * DEGTORAD;
-       if (ph < 0)
-         dasc = -dasc;
-     } else {
-       dasc = atan(sin(kv) / tan(ph));
-     }
    }
+   /* The declination of the ascendant, dasc, only ever entered this function
+    * as the product tan(dasc) * tan(ph), and the ascensional difference
+    * relation sin(kv) = tan(ph) * tan(dasc) says that product is sin(kv).
+    * Forming dasc = atan(sin(kv) / tan(ph)) and multiplying by tan(ph) again
+    * is a round trip that is 0/0 on the equator, where both sin(kv) and
+    * tan(ph) vanish. The old code broke the tie there with a constant dasc,
+    * which made the product some 2e12 times too large and returned cusps that
+    * were not the ascendant and descendant at all. Taking sin(kv) directly
+    * removes the round trip and the special case together, and agrees with
+    * the old form to a few units in the last place wherever it was defined. */
+   skv = sin(kv);
    /* note, at polar circles, when the mc sinks below the horizon,
-    * kv and dasc change sign in the above formulae.
+    * kv, and with it skv, changes sign in the above formula.
     * this is what we need, because the ascendand jumps by 180 deg */
-   /* printf("%f, %f\n", kv*RADTODEG, dasc*RADTODEG); */
+   /* printf("%f, %f\n", kv*RADTODEG, skv); */
    if (n < 8) {
      is_below_hor = 1;  /* 1 and 7 are included here */
      k = n - 1;
@@ -811,15 +813,15 @@ static double apc_sector(int n, double ph, double e, double az)
    /* az + PI/2 + kv = armc + 90 + asc. diff. = right ascension of ascendant
     * PI/2 +- kv = semi-diurnal or seminocturnal arc of ascendant 
     * a = right ascension of house cusp on apc circle (ascendant-parallel
-    * circle), with declination dasc */
+    * circle), whose declination is the ascendant's */
    if (is_below_hor) {
      a = kv + az + PI/2 + k * (PI/2 - kv) / 3;
    } else {
      a = kv + az + PI/2 + k * (PI/2 + kv) / 3;
    }
    a = swe_radnorm(a);
-   dret = atan2(tan(dasc) * tan(ph) * sin(az) + sin(a),
-      cos(e) * (tan(dasc) * tan(ph) * cos(az) + cos(a)) + sin(e) * tan(ph) * sin(az - a));
+   dret = atan2(skv * sin(az) + sin(a),
+      cos(e) * (skv * cos(az) + cos(a)) + sin(e) * tan(ph) * sin(az - a));
    dret = swe_degnorm(dret * RADTODEG);
    return dret;
 }
